everyone welcome back today I'm gonna  give you another guitar tip and today's  tip is all about sitting  [Music]  I'm always surprised that I can go to  concerts with incredibly virtuosic  musicians who are sitting in such a  contorted way that is really doing them  a disservice and even them even this  amazing performer that we can worship  over can improve could improve their  playing if they simply sat better  [Music]  the first reason we need to sit well is  so that we can breathe properly if one  has bad slouched posture the first thing  that goes is our ability to breathe  freely and if we're not breathing well  we're not playing well once we stop  breathing well our muscles tighten and  it's a chain reaction which just can  only damage our plane so let's go over  some quick tips on how you can sit  better whether you're at home practicing  or whether you're in the middle of a  concert okay so if our goal is to sit  well with an instrument we have to first  learn how to sit well without one a  basic rule that I always tell myself a  sort of mantra that you can take home  with you is head over heart heart over  hips so maybe from this angle it's  better to see if I'm slouching my head's  in front of my heart right and my heart  is in front of my hips so I can correct  this by head over heart heart over hips  and if those things aren't in line we  are not stable  we are not supported and our muscles  small muscle groups are working extra  hard to support our skeleton and instead  if we simply follow the mantra head over  heart heart over hips everything falls  into place and we can relax into this  nice supported posture which means our  muscles can work more efficiently which  means we are better performers  a really important thing to consider is  the type of chair you're using so very  often I show up to a gig and the chair  they're providing me is awful it either  has arms or it's too high too low at  home I'm just using a very simple sort  of dining chair with a cushion and it's  very important that we find the right  height of chair or use a chair that's  adjustable like a piano bench because if  our chairs too high are our legs can be  at too much of an angle and even worse  we could be tiptoeing on the ground  which causes a lot of tension through  the legs and the thing about tension is  that it translates and works its way up  and before you know it tension in your  foot can lead to tension in your fingers  if the chairs too low your knees are too  high it encourages slouching and you  can't breathe another bad chain reaction  [Music]  okay now that we're sitting well we can  add an instrument there are a number of  support devices on the market for  holding the guitar if you are just a  acoustic guitar player playing folk pop  rock something like that feel free to  simply put the guitar on the right the  right thigh in the curved section here  and leave the guitar parallel to the  ground and this is a this is a fine  playing position especially if you're  playing mostly chords in first position  it's okay just be careful it can really  encourage this sort of sitting position  so head of our heart harbor hips and you  got it another idea if you're not going  to use a guitar support is to cross the  right leg over the left leg this takes  some getting used to because for some  people who haven't done this your leg  can fall asleep but you can reposition  it and eventually you'll learn to sit in  this position for very long periods of  time flamenco guitar players for example  mostly sit like this and they can do it  for hours on end one thing I love about  crossing my leg is it brings the guitar  up a little higher which makes it easier  to play and if you sit back in the chair  it encourages a long lengthy spine and  I'm very comfortable so when I'm  teaching or just kind of hanging out and  improvising I often sit like this now if  you're a classical guitarist you're  probably going to want to hold the  guitar at more of a 45 degree angle  which gives you greater access to the  upper frets which is definitely needed  for the repertoire so the traditional  way is to use a footstool to bring the  guitar in that higher position and so by  putting the footstool on the left foot  it raises it up we put the curve of the  guitar on the left leg and the guitar  drops between our legs giving us this 45  degree angle  now I don't like the footstool  personally I have a long torso and so  for me to use a footstool I have to  raise the my knee super high to get the  guitar in a good spot so depending on  your proportions it might work great  or you might like me need to search for  a different type of device my favorite  type of guitar support is this guy it's  called an ER go play and it uses suction  cups which go onto the side of the  guitar doesn't hurt the instrument and  it really allows you to customize all of  sorts of angles that are required to get  the guitar exactly where you want it so  for me this is incredibly comfortable  you can adjust the guitar this way that  way higher or lower and the tilt which  is very important now here are some  recommendations for general rules about  how to hold the classical guitar I think  something approximating the 45 degree  angle is great although of course some  people prefer a bit more tilt some  people prefer a bit less I don't think  this is an option for classical  guitarists and so certainly not  something along this height now then  there's this angle to consider the the  tilt of the guitar I think having the  guitar vertical is less than ideal  instead I think if you let the guitar  dip back a bit against the chest it  rests directly on my sternum actually  kind of in the middle of my chest about  there and the extra bit of tilt makes it  easier for the right hand to get to the  strings and allows you to really feel  the weight of the arm on the left hand  another thing I often see is guitarists  who have one leg sticking out straight  or the the foots on the side and I would  greatly encourage you to keep both feet  flat on the ground for stability and so  that everything is in line and you can  really let go of your muscles and still  stay supported I think this allows for  the most efficient playing possible  now another thing I see often is people  trying to sort of look over the neck of  the guitar they try to get their head  here to see the front of the fretboard  and it it results in a sort of turtle  thing and you can hear my voice when I'm  when I'm speaking here and then I move  my head out here I sort of lose lose my  voice a bit and can't breathe so don't  do this it's not necessary and it's not  even helping you people think that if  they can see the front of the fretboard  that their fingers will be more accurate  but actually on the guitar with training  we don't need to see the front of the  strings we need to feel them and our  muscle memory will tell us where to land  the only thing I can really see when I'm  sitting well is I could see the top  string here and maybe the underside of  the fifth and fourth string otherwise I  can't actually see the first three  strings if you can I think that means  your guitar is too tilted or it means  you're doing the turtle thing so instead  of doing this whole turtle looking at  the guitar thing I want to encourage you  to use your eyes and some very nice free  movement from the head without moving it  in front of your body so for example if  I want to look at the the first fret I  can rotate my head to the left  tuck my chin just a little bit and then  with my eyes I can see the first fret  just fine okay so the last part of  today's tip is going to be about sitting  well even while under pressure like in a  performance setting so when we perform  we often get an adrenaline rush and it's  very common to tense our shoulders and  stop breathing so I'd encourage you  before you perform to take 10 long  breaths and to feel the the breath  coming from the belly and to pay  attention to that  swelling of the chest in the  decompression that'll help you relax a  bit and then once you're sitting well  doing the whole head of our heart heart  of her hips thing it's helpful to do two  things first is to roll your shoulders  back and let them relax the second is to  pay attention to your lower back muscles  and let those sort of let go for me  another thing that really helps us to  remember to relax the jaw so I like to  play often with my mouth slightly open  because it prevents me from clenching  and clenching the jaw shrugging the  shoulders all those stiff muscles do so  much damage to the the great technique  which you've spent countless hours  crafting so remember when performing to  breathe relax the jaw shoulders and let  those lower back muscles go alright guys  I hope you found that helpful  there will be more guitar tips coming so  stay tuned for more and if you haven't  subscribed to my youtube channel yet  please do you can see lots of new videos  coming out regularly on fiora buh lute  classical guitar and more see you later  [Music]  you
 

